  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pluteus flavofuligineus is a small, delicate mushroom in the Pluteaceae family characterized by its pale cap and distinctive coloring. This species is a wood-decomposer commonly found in New England woodlands during the growing season.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Pale to whitish cap with smoky or fulvous (tawny) tinges, particularly toward the center",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Free Gills",
      "description": "Pink to reddish gills that are free from the stem, characteristic of the Pluteus genus",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Appearance",
      "description": "Thin, smooth white to pale stem with no ring or cup-like structures at the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species fruits on decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and woody debris in mixed forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other members of Pluteus, flavofuligineus has pink spores rather than the white or brown spores found in many common mushrooms, making a spore print a helpful identification tool.",
